<%# A host putting the day view in a Sheet.

    Two things happen on one click, and neither needs a line of JavaScript
    written here: Turbo sends the response into the frame inside the sheet, and
    the `shadcn--dialog#open` action opens the sheet. The action reaches the
    controller because the calendar is rendered *inside* the Sheet's root, which
    is a `display: contents` wrapper and so costs the layout nothing.

    The built-in agenda is off *here*, because the sheet is this host's day view
    and two of them would be two answers to one question. It is not dead
    generally: it is what the Day tab will be made of once the toolbar grows a
    view switcher. Without JavaScript the day link is still a link, and the
    browser lands on the page it points at.

    The model, the route and the frame's markup are in `test/dummy` — a Lookbook
    preview can render a calendar but cannot answer its links. What comes back
    carries four fields the calendar never had: where it is, who to ring, what
    state it is in, and the note from last time. %>
<%= render(Shadcn::Sheet::Component.new) do |sheet| %>
  <% sheet.with_dialog_content(side: :right, class: "w-full sm:max-w-md",
                               "aria-label": "Day detail") do %>
    <turbo-frame id="day" class="flex flex-col gap-3 overflow-y-auto p-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Pick a day.</p>
    </turbo-frame>
  <% end %>

  <%# The calendar's own arguments come in as one local, so `density` can hand
      over whatever the toolbar is set to — month, reading, grouping, window —
      and this template only adds the three things that make the day open here:
      no built-in agenda, a link per cell, and the two attributes that make one
      click both fill the frame and open the panel.

      A default for every one of them, because this is rendered two ways:
      `density` passes locals, and `snapshot_spec` renders it with none —
      `ApplicationController.renderer.render(inline:)` has none to give. The
      golden is the default state. %>
  <% calendar = local_assigns.fetch(:calendar,
                                    { month: Date.new(2026, 8, 1),
                                      selected: Date.new(2026, 8, 20),
                                      events: Appointment.for_calendar }) %>
  <%= render(Shadcn::EventCalendar::Component.new(
        **calendar,
        agenda: false,
        day_url: ->(date) { "/calendar/days/#{date.iso8601}" },
        day_attributes: { data: { turbo_frame: "day", action: "shadcn--dialog#open" } }
      )) %>
<% end %>
